EXAMPLE 1.3. Let $\Delta = \{0, 1, 2\}$ and let $\mathcal{D} = \{\{0, 1, 2\}, \{1, 2\}, \{2\}\}$.

In pictures we have:

```mermaid
%%{init: {
  "themeCSS": ".cluster rect { rx: 80px; ry: 80px; }",
  "flowchart": { "htmlLabels": true }
}}%%
flowchart LR
  subgraph outer[" "]
    direction LR
    A["<span style='font-size:1.6em;line-height:1'>●</span><span style='font-size:1.15em'>0</span>"]
    subgraph mid[" "]
      direction LR
      B["<span style='font-size:1.6em;line-height:1'>●</span><span style='font-size:1.15em'>1</span>"]
      C(("<span style='font-size:1.6em;line-height:1'>●</span><span style='font-size:1.15em'>2</span>"))
      B ~~~ C
    end
    A ~~~ mid
  end
  style outer fill:none,stroke:#222,stroke-width:1.5px
  style mid fill:none,stroke:#222,stroke-width:1.5px
  style A fill:none,stroke:none
  style B fill:none,stroke:none
  style C fill:none,stroke:#222,stroke-width:1.5px
```

<!-- page 10 -->

Instead of stepping to the total element (here represented by 2) in one big step, the passage is divided into two steps. (Note 0 and 1 cannot be taken as representing total elements.) This example is not very interesting because the direction of approximation is unique. We need an example with some choice. $\square$

The development is with the [three-token system](1980_ThreeToken.md) peer.

<!-- formalization -->

**Theorem (`example_1_3`).** There is a neighbourhood system on $\{0,1,2\}$ with master $\Delta$ and neighbourhoods $\Delta$, $\{1,2\}$, and $\{2\}$.

*Proof.* By the three-token neighbourhood system. ∎
